15 Essential Kubernetes Commands for Every Engineer

🔍 Phase 1: Cluster & Resource Inspection
Before making changes, you need to see the current state of your environment.

Check Cluster Nodes

kubectl get nodes

Displays all nodes and their status (e.g., Ready/NotReady) to ensure the infrastructure is healthy.

View Running Pods

kubectl get pods

The go-to command for checking the status of your applications in the current namespace.

View Pods with Detailed Info

kubectl get pods -o wide

Adds crucial context like the Pod IP and which Node the pod is scheduled on.

Check Services (Networking)

kubectl get svc

Lists services to verify ClusterIPs, NodePorts, or LoadBalancer status.
Check Deployments

kubectl get deployments

Shows the desired vs. actual state of your application instances.

Get All Resources

kubectl get all

A "bird’s eye view" that shows pods, services, deployments, and replica sets simultaneously.

🛠️ Phase 2: Troubleshooting & Debugging

When things go wrong, use these commands to find the "Why."
Describe a Pod (The Gold Standard)
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Essential for seeing events (like ImagePullBackOff or OOMKilled) and container lifecycle details.

View Pod Logs

kubectl logs <pod-name>

Retrieves the application's stdout/stderr logs to find code-level errors.

Stream Logs in Real-Time
kubectl logs -f <pod-name>

Follows the logs live—perfect for monitoring a crash or a startup sequence.

Logs for Specific Containers

kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>

Used when a Pod has multiple containers (sidecars) and you need to isolate one.

View Cluster Events

kubectl get events

 --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
Provides a chronological timeline of everything happening in the cluster.

🚀 Phase 3: Operations & Interaction
These commands are used to apply changes or interact directly with the workload.

Apply Configuration

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

The standard way to create or update resources using declarative YAML files.

Execute a Shell Inside a Pod

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- /bin/bash

Allows you to "ssh" into a container to check files, environment variables, or network connectivity.

Scale a Deployment

kubectl scale deployment <name> --replicas=5

Instantly increases or decreases the number of running pods to handle traffic.

Delete Resources

kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml

Gracefully removes all resources defined in a specific file.

💡 The "Golden Rule" of K8s Troubleshooting

If an application isn't working, always follow this order:
get pods (Is it running?)
describe pod (If it's not running, did the cluster fail to start it?)
logs (If it is running but crashing, what does the application say?)
Mastering this sequence will save you hours of "poking around" in the dark!
